  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nSynoptically, a broad H5 ridge remains stretched across the eastern\nhalf of the CONUS, ushering in a seasonably warm air mass throughout \nthe Ohio Valley. This ridge will dampen and result in more quasi- \nzonal flow aloft as we progress into the middle of the week, but no \nnotable changes at the surface will be realized. For the entirety of \nthe work week, temperatures will trend above seasonal normals, with \novernight lows becoming more mild late week into the weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nGlobal models and ensembles have been persistent in showing a shift \nin the weather pattern across the Ohio Valley this weekend. This is \nin response to a negatively tilted trough that swings through \nportions of the Midwest region. A plume of moisture will be ingested \nahead of this trough, resulting in more cloud coverage on Saturday \nand higher dewpoints that will keep overnight lows in the middle to \nupper 50s Saturday night. The best forcing appears to arrive on \nSunday, resulting in the highest PoPs for our CWA. Still far enough \nout that timing details aren't totally worked out, but note that we \ncould observe rain as early as Saturday evening depending on the \nprogression of this shortwave trough.",
